  "notes": [
    {
      "title": "A complete illustration carries its assumptions",
      "text": "An illustration is labeled as life insurance and includes identifying policy, insurer, producer and proposed-insured information. Guaranteed values are clearly distinguished from nonguaranteed values. The latter cannot be described as certain, and an incomplete illustration is prohibited.",
      "example": "Showing only the attractive nonguaranteed column conceals the information needed to understand the projection.",
      "topics": [
        "3.8.03",
        "3.8.05"
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Revised terms require a revised presentation",
      "text": "COMAR 31.09.09.09 distinguishes a policy applied for as illustrated, one issued on different terms, and an application without a conforming illustration. The proper certification, signatures, copies and delivery process depend on that situation. The insurer retains the applicable signed illustrations and certifications until three years after the policy is no longer in force; no copy is required under that paragraph if no policy is issued.",
      "example": "A higher issued premium requires a presentation that matches the issued contract, not the old estimate.",
      "topics": [
        "3.8.03",
        "1.2.04"
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "The summary explains the particular policy",
      "text": "For covered forms not marketed with an illustration, the summary shows guarantees only. Required information includes the carrier and producer details, policy and rider types, specified premium and benefit patterns, guaranteed cash surrender values and applicable loan-interest information. Costs are shown as totals rather than only per-unit figures.",
      "example": "A customer comparing two policies needs the actual annual costs and benefits, not simply the smallest quoted cost per thousand dollars.",
      "topics": [
        "3.8.04",
        "3.8.05"
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Initial and annual notices are different",
      "text": "An initial notice generally accompanies establishment of the continuing customer relationship, subject to the regulation’s specific exceptions. Annual notice has a separate exception when only the specified permitted disclosure categories are used and the previously disclosed policies and practices have not changed. A former customer without a continuing relationship does not require the annual notice under this rule.",
      "example": "Do not answer that a notice is always required every year regardless of the exception facts.",
      "topics": [
        "1.2.12",
        "3.8.09"
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "An opt-out opportunity must be meaningful",
      "text": "Before disclosures governed by COMAR 31.16.08.11, provide the required initial and opt-out notices, allow a reasonable opportunity and respect the consumer’s decision. The rule gives examples, including a thirty-day response period for mailed notices. Specific servicing, legal and other exceptions have their own conditions.",
      "example": "Selling a client list to an unrelated marketer is a different purpose from sending information needed to service the client’s requested policy.",
      "topics": [
        "1.2.12",
        "3.8.07"
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Life underwriting can involve health information",
      "text": "This remains a life-only course. Health information appears because a life application can involve medical facts. COMAR 31.16.08.17 supplies an authorization rule and enumerated insurance-function exceptions. Other applicable laws and consent rules must also be considered; this single exception list does not settle every privacy question.",
      "example": "Use the authorized underwriting channel to transmit requested records instead of sharing them with unrelated sales staff.",
      "topics": [
        "1.2.12",
        "3.8.07"
      ]
    }
  ],
